---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav from '../components/Nav.astro';
import FooterBack from '../components/FooterBack.astro';
import Code from '../components/Code.astro';
import ManRef from '../components/ManRef.astro';
import { buildFromSource, docsJob, docsRestart } from '../data/snippets';
import '../styles/docs.css';
import '../styles/docs-page.css';
import { kwRows } from '../lib/reference.mjs';
import { entryHref, entryMap } from '../lib/manual-pages.mjs';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const entry = (id) => entryHref(id, null, base);
// Diagnose entries of the manual, labelled by their manual headings
const diagnose = ['diag-wrong-window', 'diag-maxiter', 'diag-oscillation', 'diag-fon-inverted', 'diag-fon-pinned',
  'diag-localized', 'diag-unknown-token', 'diag-catalog', 'diag-ghost-states', 'diag-quasi-degenerate']
  .map((id) => [id, entryMap().get(id).title]);
const navLinks = [
  { href: base, label: 'Overview' },
  { href: '#install', label: 'Install' },
  { href: '#running', label: 'Running a job' },
  { href: '#keywords', label: 'Keyword reference' },
  { href: `${base}manual/`, label: 'User manual' },
];
const toc = [
  ['install', 'Installation'],
  ['running', 'Running a job'],
  ['keywords', 'Keyword reference'],
  ['active-space', 'Active space'],
  ['naming', 'Config names & tokens'],
  ['sa', 'SA ensemble'],
  ['si', 'SI ensemble'],
  ['spin', 'Spin manifolds (2S > 0)'],
  ['response', 'Gradients & couplings'],
  ['restart', 'Guesses & restarts'],
  ['fon', 'Fractional occupations'],
  ['deloc', 'Delocalization penalty'],
  ['solver', 'Solver & convergence'],
  ['reporting', 'Reporting'],
  ['troubleshooting', 'Troubleshooting'],
];
---
<Base title="Documentation — REKS Studio">
<Nav variant="light" logoHref={base} links={navLinks} />

<header class="docshero">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="kicker">Documentation</div>
    <h1>Installing REKS Studio and configuring an ensemble</h1>
    <p>A working reference for building the package and controlling the state-averaged and state-interaction ensembles it runs. For the method itself — why REKS Studio exists and how it stays fast — see the <a href={base} style="text-decoration:underline;">overview page</a>.</p>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="wrap">
  <div class="docs-layout">
    <details class="toc">
      <summary>Contents</summary>
      <nav class="toc-list">{toc.map(([id, label]) => <a href={`#${id}`}>{label}</a>)}</nav>
    </details>

    <div>
      <section id="install" class="docsec">
        <h2>Installation</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>REKS Studio is currently a development branch of Psi4, not yet a standalone package or conda release. Building it follows Psi4's own CMake-based build process. The configuration catalogues are shipped as binary files in <code>psi4/share/psi4/reks_catalog/</code> and installed with Psi4 — nothing extra to run afterward.</p>
        </div>
        <Code code={buildFromSource} lang="bash" />
        <div class="note"><strong>Note.</strong> This branch is research software under active development. Interfaces and keyword names may change before it's merged upstream.</div>
      </section>

      <section id="running" class="docsec">
        <h2>Running a job <ManRef id="overview" /></h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Once built, REKS runs as an ordinary Psi4 reference — no separate binary or import. Set <code>reference</code> to <code>reks</code>, give the active space as <code>[N, M]</code>, and call <code>psi4.energy()</code> as usual.</p>
        </div>
        <Code code={docsJob} lang="python" />
      </section>

      <section id="keywords" class="docsec">
        <h2>Keyword reference</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Keywords marked with an alias have a <code>REKS_*</code>-prefixed second name (e.g. <code>sa_reks_configs</code> / <code>reks_sa_configs</code>) — set one or the other, never both. Defaults are noted where a keyword can be left out entirely.</p>
        </div>

        <div id="active-space" class="kwgroup">
          <h3>Reference & active space <ManRef id="active-space" /></h3>
          <div class="kwlist" set:html={kwRows(['reference', 'REKS', 'REKS_GEMINAL_WIDTHS'])} />
          <div class="note"><strong>Note.</strong> Each active space is a pre-generated catalogue: <code>(2,2)</code>, <code>(4,4)</code>, <code>(6,6)</code>, and with <code>reks_geminal_widths</code> <code>(2,3)[3]</code>, <code>(2,4)[4]</code>, <code>(4,5)[2,3]</code>, <code>(4,6)[3,3]</code>. Any other <code>(N, M)</code> fails at load time.</div>
        </div>

        <div id="naming" class="kwgroup">
          <h3>Configuration names & bulk tokens <ManRef id="catalog" /></h3>
          <div class="kwgroup-note">Every configuration in the catalogue has a name of the form <code>&lt;TYPE&gt;&lt;index&gt;</code>, e.g. <code>PPS1</code>, <code>OSS2</code>, <code>T_PPS3</code>; a member of a spin-polarized set is named <code>&lt;TYPE&gt;_&lt;k&gt;</code>, e.g. <code>DOSS_SPS_0</code>. You rarely need the full list — an unknown name in a config keyword makes REKS Studio print every valid name and type of the manifold the block is bound to — but the type prefixes are worth knowing:</div>
          <div class="kwlist" set:html={kwRows(['pps', 'oss-doss-toss', 'des-ddes-tdes', 't-tt-ttt-prefix', 'sps-tag'])} />
          <div class="kwgroup-note" style="margin-top:22px;">Anywhere a config name is expected — in <code>sa_reks_configs</code> or <code>si_reks_configs</code> — these bulk tokens also work, so you rarely have to spell out individual names:</div>
          <div class="kwlist" set:html={kwRows(['full', 'a-type-name', 'single-scheme-n', 'mixed-scheme-n', 'sps', 'a-pattern', 'exclude-x'])} />
          <div class="note"><strong>Note.</strong> A PSithon (<code>.dat</code>) input can't use a literal <code>:</code> inside an array element — write scheme indices with <code>-</code> or <code>_</code> instead, e.g. <code>single-scheme-1-2</code>.</div>
        </div>

        <div id="sa" class="kwgroup">
          <h3>State-averaged (SA) ensemble <ManRef id="sa-pool" /></h3>
          <div class="kwgroup-note">The configurations whose orbitals are jointly optimized in the SCF step.</div>
          <div class="kwlist" set:html={kwRows(['SA_REKS_CONFIGS', 'SA_REKS_2SPIN', 'SA_REKS_WEIGHTS', 'SA_REKS_EXTRA'])} />
        </div>

        <div id="si" class="kwgroup">
          <h3>State-interaction (SI) ensemble <ManRef id="si-cassettes" /></h3>
          <div class="kwgroup-note">Defined independently of the SA pool — mixed afterward to produce the final multi-state energies.</div>
          <div class="kwlist" set:html={kwRows(['SI_REKS_CONFIGS', 'SI_REKS_2SPIN', 'SI_REKS_PRINT_STATES', 'SI_REKS_STORE_STATES', 'SI_REKS_ANALYSIS', 'SI_REKS_ANALYSIS_CHANGE_MIN', 'SI_REKS_OVERLAP_CUTOFF'])} />
        </div>

        <div id="spin" class="kwgroup">
          <h3>Spin manifolds (2S &gt; 0) <ManRef id="spin-manifolds" /></h3>
          <div class="kwgroup-note">Every configuration is the total M<sub>S</sub> = 0 component of its manifold, so one set of orbitals serves every manifold. A run's sectors are the union of the 2S values declared by SA blocks and SI groups.</div>
          <div class="kwlist" set:html={kwRows(['high-spin-only', 'several-manifolds', 'state-labels', 'output'])} />
        </div>

        <div id="response" class="kwgroup">
          <h3>Gradients, couplings & relaxed properties <ManRef id="response" /></h3>
          <div class="kwgroup-note">Analytic SI-state gradients and nonadiabatic couplings via the coupled-perturbed REKS (CP-REKS) response. List-of-lists options mirror <code>si_reks_configs</code>: inner list <code>e</code> refers to cassette <code>e</code>.</div>
          <div class="kwlist" set:html={kwRows(['SI_REKS_GRAD', 'SI_REKS_NAC', 'SI_REKS_PROPERTY_FOR', 'REKS_EKT', 'REKS_CPREKS_CONV', 'REKS_GRAD_DEGEN_TOL', 'REKS_CI_TOPOGRAPHY_GAP'])} />
        </div>

        <div id="restart" class="kwgroup">
          <h3>Guesses & restarts <ManRef id="guesses" /></h3>
          <div class="kwgroup-note">REKS jobs converge far more reliably from a previous wavefunction than from a cold guess, so most production runs are chained restarts. Restarting uses ordinary Psi4 machinery; the keyword below selects which read orbitals form the active space.</div>
          <Code code={docsRestart} lang="python" />
          <div class="kwlist" set:html={kwRows(['GUESS_ACTIVE_WINDOW'])} />
          <div class="note"><strong>Tip.</strong> If SCF converges instantly to a suspiciously wrong energy right after a restart, this is almost always the symptom — check the Molden output, then fix the active window rather than the whole guess.</div>
        </div>

        <div id="fon" class="kwgroup">
          <h3>Fractional occupation numbers (FONs) <ManRef id="fons" /></h3>
          <div class="kwgroup-note">Each geminal pair carries FONs <code>(n_p, n_q)</code> with <code>n_p + n_q = 2</code>, optimized every SCF iteration with <code>n_p</code> in <code>[floor, 2 − margin]</code>.</div>
          <div class="kwlist" set:html={kwRows(['REKS_FON_INTERP_DELTA', 'REKS_N_FON'])} />
        </div>

        <div id="deloc" class="kwgroup">
          <h3>Delocalization penalty <ManRef id="ipr" /></h3>
          <div class="kwgroup-note">An optional energy term <code>E_pen = λ Σ<sub>i</sub> Σ<sub>A</sub> p<sub>A</sub>(i)²</code> added to the SA energy, with <code>p<sub>A</sub>(i)</code> the population of active MO <code>i</code> on atom <code>A</code>. A positive λ penalizes localization and drives the SCF to the delocalized solution, the intended REKS solution: <code>Σ p²</code> decreases (H<sub>4</sub>, REKS(4,4): 1.1128 at λ = 0, 1.0669 at λ = 0.05).</div>
          <div class="kwlist" set:html={kwRows(['REKS_DELOC_IPR_PENALTY', 'REKS_DELOC_IPR_METHOD'])} />
        </div>

        <div id="solver" class="kwgroup">
          <h3>Solver & convergence <ManRef id="scf-engines" /></h3>
          <div class="kwgroup-note">Three cooperating engines drive REKS's orbital/FON optimization — a plain level-shifted burn-in, an optional trust-region warmup, and the GVB-DIIS accelerator that normally finishes the job. Defaults work well for most systems; most users only ever touch <code>level_shift</code>.</div>
          <div class="kwlist" set:html={kwRows(['REKS_USE_TRAH', 'REKS_GVB_DIIS', 'REKS_DIIS_FORMULATION', 'REKS_GVB_DIIS_START', 'LEVEL_SHIFT_ADAPT'])} />
          <div class="note"><strong>Note.</strong> 23 further <code>reks_diis_*</code> / <code>reks_gvb_*</code> options (angle filter, SVD conditioning, rewind and restart monitors) are expert settings; see the <a href={entry('option-table')}>manual</a>. <code>reks_report_level 4</code> shows which monitor fires.</div>
        </div>

        <div id="reporting" class="kwgroup">
          <h3>Reporting <ManRef id="report-level" /></h3>
          <div class="kwlist" set:html={kwRows(['REKS_REPORT_LEVEL'])} />
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="troubleshooting" class="docsec">
        <h2>Troubleshooting <ManRef id="diagnose" /></h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Each symptom links to its entry in the manual: what the output prints, then what to do.</p>
          <ul>{diagnose.map(([id, label]) => <li><a href={entry(id)}>{label}</a></li>)}</ul>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<FooterBack href={base} label="Back to REKS Studio overview" />
</Base>
